plus dual write and \\r\\nShadow comparison; new caches can start directly at V2. Register each Key in `RedisKeySpec` with Type/Codec/Schema, and\\r\\n never mix `StringCodec` and `JsonJacksonCodec` on one Key. Snapshot V2 needs same-tag formal/staging/manifest/fence; o\\r\\nrdinary C2 caches do not need the full snapshot protocol. [Task 2]\\r\\n- For compatible additive payload changes, stay in the current key namespace and handle compatibility through `schemaVe\\r\\nrsion`; use `v2` → `v3` only for destructive changes. When retiring V1, explicitly clean the old namespace, manifest, a\\r\\nnd fence by domain to avoid `noeviction` capacity leakage. [Task 2]\\r\\n- Slow cache-invalidation Outbox consumption is intentional in this checkout: `CacheInvalidationOutboxProcessor` has `B\\r\\nATCH_SIZE=200`, `@Scheduled(fixedDelay=60_000)`, and oldest-first scanning, so 1577 PENDING needs at least eight cycles\\r\\n (about eight minutes plus work); ~8800 records need about 44 minutes under the same ceiling. Actual cycle time is proc\\r\\nessing plus the fixed 60-second delay. [Task 3]\\r\\n- Each item is serial `tryClaim` (`REQUIRES_NEW`) → MySQL fact read/Redis staging+formal/Lua RENAME/manifest/fence-CAS \\r\\nprojection → `markCompleted` (`REQUIRES_NEW`), so DB/Redis round trips dominate. PENDING disappearing after claim means\\r\\n PROCESSING, not completed; retries use `min(60*2^N,3600)` and future `NEXT_RETRY_TIME` records are not scanned. `LEASE\\r\\n_DURATION_SECONDS=120`; acceptance remains `outbox_pending_oldest_seconds<120s` and `FAILED=0`. Safe semantic-preservin\\r\\ng accelerators are larger batch, shorter delay, or more instances because `tryClaim` atomically prevents duplicate owne\\r\\nrship. [Task 3]\\r\\n\\r\\n## Failures and how to do differently\\r\\n\\r\\n- Symptom: a SCAN pattern returns 0 although the code writes the domain. Cause: the key pattern or hash-tag placement i\\r\\ns wrong. Fix: first broad-scan (`group:*:member*`) and inspect the real key